export const DE_B2_S07_ENGLISH: Record<string, string[]> = {
  "de-der-ghostwriter": [
    `Jonas Petzold had studied German literature, started two novels and finished neither of them. For a year he had been earning his money with a job he preferred not to mention at parties: he wrote love messages for other people. Anyone on a dating app who didn't know what to write could book him through a plain website, thirty euros per message, discretion guaranteed. His clients were surprisingly normal: dentists, teachers, a policeman from Halle who supposedly only needed "a bit of help getting started".`,
    `In March two new clients got in touch within a week. Kai, a tram driver from Connewitz, wrote that he was "more the quiet type" with women and couldn't put a sensible sentence together on a screen. Shortly afterwards he was booked by Sabine, a vet from Gohlis, who openly admitted that her messages so far had been "about as romantic as a vaccination record". Jonas accepted both jobs without thinking much about it.`,
    `He only noticed that something was wrong after ten days. Kai sent him the latest message from his new acquaintance so that Jonas could write a suitable reply. Jonas read it and stopped short. He knew the sentence about the fog on Lake Cospuden, which looked as if someone had forgotten to finish painting the sky. He had written it himself, the evening before, for Sabine.`,
    `He sat in front of his laptop for a long time. So for a week and a half he had been carrying on a correspondence with himself, and quite a good one at that. The two people paying for it had fallen in love with a tone that neither of them had ever struck themselves. Worse still: by now both of them wanted to meet. "What on earth do I say when we're sitting across from each other?" Kai had asked. Sabine wanted to know whether Jonas couldn't just write her a little cheat sheet.`,
    `It would have been honest to tell them both everything. It would have been easy simply to keep on writing. Jonas chose a third way, without knowing himself whether it was wise. He sent them both the same message: from now on he would no longer write anything for them. If they wanted to meet, they should do so, but in their own words, however clumsy those might be. He refunded the money for the last week.`,
    `The following Saturday he sat in a café on the market square, a newspaper in front of his face like a bad detective, two tables away from the place the two of them had agreed on. Kai came too early, Sabine too late. The first few minutes were agony. Kai talked about timetables, Sabine about a guinea pig with dental problems. There was no mention of fog or unfinished skies. Jonas would have loved to step in.`,
    `Then Sabine suddenly said, "Can I confess something? I didn't actually write those messages myself." Kai went red, stared into his coffee and then laughed so loudly that people turned round. "Neither did I," he said. "I paid someone to do it." "So did I!" They looked at each other, and for the first time that afternoon they seemed really to see each other. Jonas quietly folded up his newspaper and left before either of them had the idea of looking around.`,
    `Three months later he got a short message from Kai: "We're together now. Sabine says you were good but we're better. From now on we write ourselves even if it doesn't sound so nice." Jonas read the lines twice. Three commas were missing and there wasn't a single metaphor, and yet it was, he thought, the best thing he had read in a long time. That same evening he took one of his unfinished novels out of the drawer.`,
  ],
  "de-die-alte-bestzeit": [
    `In her father's notebook the number stood on the last page with writing on it, underlined twice: 7:42. Below it, in his angular handwriting: "Albula Tour 1989. Next year under seven hours!" For Urs Caduff there had been no next year. He had died the following winter in an avalanche on the Flüela Pass, when his daughter Nadine was four years old. She had only found the notebook as an adult, in a box in her mother's cellar in Chur, between old cycling jerseys and a dented water bottle.`,
    `Nadine was not a sportswoman. She worked as a pharmacist, took the bus to work and had, as she said herself, the fitness of a sofa cushion. But the number wouldn't let her go. For four years she trained, secretly at first, then with growing ambition. She got up at five, struggled up to Lenzerheide and learned what gear ratio, cadence and hitting the wall meant. Her goal was modest and enormous at the same time: she wanted to complete the tour just once in less than 7 hours 42. Not for herself, she said, but for him.`,
    `On the day of the race the sky over the Engadine was cloudless. Nadine set off carefully, as her coach had drummed into her, and after two passes she was well on schedule. On the Albula Pass, on the last hairpin bends below the summit, she saw an older man sitting at the side of the road, next to him a racing bike with a broken chain. Hundreds of riders passed him by. Nadine braked, although she knew that every minute counted.`,
    `The man's name was Gian Lutz; he was almost seventy and had no tools with him. Nadine did. While she mended the chain with blackened fingers, he asked where she was from. When she told him her surname, he paused. "Caduff? Not Urs's daughter, surely?" He had ridden with her father back then, he told her, in 1989, exactly this route. They had been friends since their apprenticeship.`,
    `Nadine told him about the notebook and the number she had been chasing for four years. Gian looked at her for a long time, then laughed quietly and shook his head. "7:42? He wrote that down?" Her father, he said, hadn't officially finished at all that year. On the Julier Pass a young rider had crashed, and Urs had stayed with him until the ambulance came, almost an hour. After that he had ridden leisurely to the end, long out of the official results. The 7:42 was presumably the time he would have had if he hadn't stopped. "That's how your father did his sums. Always a bit generous with himself."`,
    `Nadine didn't know whether to laugh or cry, so she did both. For four years she had been chasing a time that had never existed. At the same time she felt she was getting to know her father properly for the first time: a man who would rather sit in the dust next to a stranger than ride a best time, and who afterwards still didn't want to give up his number entirely.`,
    `The chain held. Gian and Nadine rode the rest of the route together, without hurrying, and on every descent Gian told a new story about Urs: how he had once cycled over the Bernina with a whole wheel of cheese in his rucksack, how he had sung on every pass, out of tune and loudly. At the finish in Zuoz the clock showed 8:03. Twenty-one minutes too slow.`,
    `That evening Nadine opened the notebook. Under the twice-underlined number she wrote in her own round handwriting: "Albula Tour, second attempt by the Caduff family. 8:03, with a break. Under seven hours: next year!" Then she phoned Gian and asked him whether he would ride with her again next year. This time, she said, she would bring two spare chains.`,
  ],
  "de-die-kasse-des-vertrauens": [
    `Anyone heading for Wildenfelde, a village of 140 inhabitants in the north of the Uckermark, has to be prepared for cobblestones, long tree-lined avenues and rather unreliable mobile reception. There has been no baker here since 1998, and the nearest bus stop with more than two departures a day is eight kilometres away. Since last spring, however, there has been something the people of Wildenfelde proudly call "our shop": a converted wooden hut next to the fire station, with no till, no camera and no staff.`,
    `The principle could hardly be simpler. Customers take milk, eggs, pasta or toilet paper from the shelves, write the goods down in a notebook and drop the money into a locked metal box. Change lies in a biscuit tin next to it. The idea comes from the honorary mayor, Ralf Krüger, 58, a farmer by profession. At the beginning he expected losses, he says, ten to fifteen per cent. They had decided to accept that as the price of community.`,
    `Things turned out differently. Since the very first month the accounts haven't added up, but in the wrong direction. There is regularly more money in the box than the entries in the notebook come to. Sometimes it's twenty euros, sometimes thirty-five; in December it was as much as ninety. Krüger has checked the figures several times, so has his wife, and finally even the auditor of the sports club. A mistake in the sums, everyone agreed, was out of the question.`,
    `Since then the wildest theories have been going round the village. Some suspect the pastor wants to set a good example, which he firmly denies. Others believe in a rich Berliner with a weekend house who is easing his bad conscience. The primary school children are convinced it's a ghost. Krüger himself at first wanted to let the matter rest. "Who complains about too much money?" At some point, though, he admits, curiosity got the better of him.`,
    `The answer, as so often, was in the small print. One morning, between the banknotes, there was a carefully folded note. On it, in small handwriting, was written: "Instalment 14. For Frau Behrendt. Sweets, chewing gum, comics, 1986 to 1989." Margot Behrendt, as everyone over fifty in the village knew, had run the Konsum until reunification, the little cooperative village shop that had stood exactly where the hut stands today. She died six years ago.`,
    `It took another two weeks before the anonymous payer got in touch with our newspaper. He does not want to be named, lives in Potsdam today and works as a tax adviser, which he himself calls "a certain irony". As a boy, he says, he spent his summers with his grandmother in Wildenfelde, and during them he stole from the Konsum "systematically and with great talent". Frau Behrendt knew, he is sure of that today. She never said anything. She just pressed one more lollipop into his hand every time he left.`,
    `When he heard about the shop without a shopkeeper on the radio, he worked it out: around three hundred East German marks, generously rounded up to a thousand euros with interest and a guilty conscience. Since then he has driven out once a month to pay an instalment. "I would have liked to give it back to her in person," he says. "That's not possible any more. But the shop stands in the same place, and that comes pretty close."`,
    `Mayor Krüger has meanwhile paid the extra money into a separate account. Once the debt has been paid off, the plan is to use it to buy a bench in front of the hut, with a small brass plaque. What it should say is still disputed. The children suggested "For Frau Behrendt", says Krüger. The tax adviser from Potsdam asked for something else: "For everyone who knew and said nothing."`,
  ],
  "de-der-grantige-fiaker": [
    `Ferdinand Brandstätter had been driving a Fiaker, a horse-drawn cab, in Vienna for thirty-one years, and in all those years he had earned a reputation he was proud of in his own way: he was considered the unfriendliest coachman on the Stephansplatz. Tourists who asked for Mozart's house were told they had just walked past it, "like everyone". Selfie sticks, outdoor jackets and loudly asked questions about the price he answered with a sigh that replaced whole paragraphs. His mare Burgi, a Haflinger with a stoic gaze, was the only one he really talked to.`,
    `One hot July afternoon a young woman with a ring light and a phone tripod climbed into his carriage. Her name was Lea, she came from Düsseldorf and had, as she informed him, "just under four hundred thousand followers, mostly in travel and lifestyle". Did he mind if she filmed a bit? Ferdinand certainly did mind, but he only said, "Film away, then. The horse is more photogenic than me anyway."`,
    `The ride lasted forty minutes. Lea asked whether anyone actually still lived in the Hofburg. "Only civil servants," said Ferdinand, "and you don't notice them." She asked why the Viennese were supposedly so grumpy. "We're not grumpy," he replied, "we just don't feel like pretending we're pleased." When she asked him to smile once for her followers, he looked at her as if she had asked him to ring the cathedral bells with his own hands.`,
    `Three days later half of Germany knew his face. The video, titled "The most honest man in Vienna", had been clicked on more than eight million times. At the Stephansplatz queues formed in front of his carriage while his colleagues next door twiddled their thumbs. People didn't want to see Vienna at all. They wanted to be insulted, preferably on camera. A family from Stuttgart explicitly asked him to say "that thing about the civil servants again".`,
    `In those weeks Ferdinand earned more than in the whole previous year, and he was unhappier than ever. Grumpiness on demand, he felt, was no longer grumpiness but theatre, and bad theatre at that. So out of sheer defiance he started being friendly. He patiently explained the difference between Gothic and Baroque, praised outdoor jackets and let children stroke Burgi. The comments online were devastating: the man had "gone soft", was "not real any more", a "fake".`,
    `At the end of September Lea was standing in front of his carriage again, this time without a ring light. She had read what had happened, she said, and she was sorry. She hadn't thought a single video could do something like that. Ferdinand looked her over for a while. Then he pointed at the seat with his chin. "Get in. But the phone stays in your bag."`,
    `This time he didn't drive the usual round. He showed her the back courtyard where his grandfather had changed the horses, the little Beisl, a traditional pub, where he had met his wife, and a bench by the Danube Canal where he ate his sausage sandwich every evening while Burgi drank from a bucket. He talked more than in the previous thirty-one years put together, or at least that's how it felt to him.`,
    `At the end Lea asked what the truth was, then: was he a grumpy person or not? Ferdinand thought for a long time while Burgi clip-clopped leisurely over the cobbles. "You know," he said at last, "Viennese grumpiness is really just shyness with better posture." Lea laughed. She didn't write the sentence down, and she never posted it. But the next summer, so they say at the Stephansplatz, she came back, and she wouldn't get into any other coachman's carriage.`,
  ],
  "de-das-konto-4471-b": [
    `In a windowless office on the third basement floor of a private bank in Zurich, Reto Brunner worked on cases that were rarely talked about outside the building. His department dealt with so-called dormant assets: accounts whose owners hadn't been in touch for decades, neither in person nor by post. After sixty years without a sign of life, the law required the bank to search for the heirs. If it found none, the money went to the federal government.`,
    `Account 4471-B had been opened in May 1966 by one Rosa Gerber, twenty-two at the time, occupation: waitress. She had paid in four thousand eight hundred francs, a small fortune for a young waitress from the Emmental. After that nothing had ever been paid in or withdrawn. With interest, the account now held almost thirty-one thousand francs. The file also contained a yellowed index card on which was written, in round handwriting: "For my child, if it asks."`,
    `Reto had seen many files, but this one wouldn't let him go. He searched registers, wrote to local councils in the Emmental and phoned a retired pastor in Langnau who actually remembered Rosa. She had become pregnant in 1965 without being married, the old man told him, which in the village at that time had been a scandal. People had pushed her to give the child up for adoption. Shortly afterwards she had emigrated to Canada. Rosa herself, as Reto found out later, had died in Winnipeg in 2011, without any other children.`,
    `Finding the adoption file cost him another four months and a considerable number of forms. When the reply from the relevant authority finally came, he read the name three times before he believed it. The girl, born in Bern in February 1966, had been adopted by a couple from Oerlikon and was now called Ines Keller.`,
    `Reto knew Ines Keller. Everyone in the bank knew her, although hardly anyone knew her surname. For fourteen years she had cleaned the offices on the third basement floor in the evenings, his among them. She emptied his wastepaper basket, watered the plant he kept forgetting, and said "Have a nice evening, Herr Brunner" every time he finally took his coat at half past six. He had never exchanged more than a few sentences about the weather with her.`,
    `He could have sent her a registered letter, as the regulations recommended. Instead, one Tuesday, he waited until after seven. When she came in with her cleaning trolley, he asked her to sit down for a moment. She looked at him suspiciously, as if she expected a complaint about the plant. Then, as calmly as he could, he told her about the account, about Rosa Gerber, about the Emmental and about Winnipeg.`,
    `Ines listened in silence. She had always known she was adopted, she said at last. Her parents had never hidden it from her, and she couldn't have been luckier with them. But about the woman who had given birth to her she had wondered only one thing her whole life: whether she had wanted her at all. Reto said nothing. He pushed the copy of the index card across the table to her.`,
    `She read the short sentence for a long time. She didn't ask about the money once that evening. She only wanted to know whether she could have the original card, with her mother's handwriting. Strictly speaking, said Reto, it was the bank's property, and internal documents were not allowed to leave the building as a matter of principle. Then he took the card out of the file, put it in an envelope and handed it to her. "I think," he said, "it was addressed to you from the start."`,
  ],
};

